# Frontend Architecture & File Organization Rules

Follow these rules when creating, moving, or refactoring frontend code in this Next.js application.

The primary architectural principle is:

> **Organize code by ownership and scope, not by file type.**

A component, hook, type, constant, helper, schema, or action should live at the **narrowest scope that correctly owns it**.

Do not create global `utils/`, `helpers/`, `types/`, `constants/`, or similar directories merely because those categories exist.

---

## This repository

- Paths are **at the repo root** (no `src/`): `app/`, `components/`, `lib/`, `hooks/`.
- There is **no** `features/` tree yet. Add `features/<domain>/` only when a real domain boundary exists (multiple routes, coherent business capability).
- Examples today: `app/(app)/trading-dashboard/` (`_components/dashboard|chart/`, `_lib/chart|overlays|bias/`), shared UI in `components/ui/`, market API pipeline in `lib/ticks/`, auth in `lib/supabase/`.
- **Do not mass-reorganize** existing files for style alone (see §20). Apply these rules to **new** code and low-risk colocation when you are already modifying an area.

Import aliases use `@/` (e.g. `@/components/...`, `@/lib/...`).

---

## 1. Default Next.js structure

Use the Next.js App Router.

A representative structure (this repo uses root-level `app/`, not `src/app/`):

```text
app/
├── (auth)/
│   ├── login/
│   │   ├── page.tsx
│   │   └── _components/
│   │       └── LoginForm.tsx
│   └── register/
│       ├── page.tsx
│       └── _components/
│           └── RegisterForm.tsx
├── dashboard/
│   ├── page.tsx
│   ├── loading.tsx
│   ├── _components/
│   ├── _hooks/
│   ├── types.ts
│   └── constants.ts
├── layout.tsx
└── globals.css

components/
├── ui/
└── layout/

hooks/
lib/
features/          # only when a real domain boundary exists
stores/
providers/
types/
```

This is a **reference**, not a requirement to create every directory. Do not create empty directories or placeholders.

---

## 2. Scope hierarchy

```text
Page / route → Domain / feature → Application-wide shared
```

Always start with the narrowest scope.

### Decision tree

```text
Only needed by one route/page? → app/<route>/

Shared by multiple pages in one domain? → features/<domain>/

Genuinely generic/application-wide? → components/, hooks/, lib/, stores/, providers/, types/, etc.
```

Do not move code global because it might be reused someday.

---

## 3. Page-owned components

Prefer:

```text
app/users/
├── page.tsx
└── _components/
    ├── UserTable.tsx
    └── UserFilters.tsx
```

over `components/users/...` when those components are only for `/users`.

`_` prefix = private route implementation. Promote upward only when ownership genuinely broadens.

---

## 4. Page-owned hooks

Route-only hooks live with the route:

```text
app/users/_hooks/useUserFilters.ts
```

Global `hooks/` is for **generic** React behavior only (`useDebounce`, `useMediaQuery`, `usePrevious`, etc.).

---

## 5. Page-owned types

Page-specific types beside the page:

```text
app/users/types.ts
```

Avoid `app/users/_types/` unless complexity warrants a directory. Avoid `types/` at root for page-only types.

---

## 6. Domain / feature scope

Use `features/<domain>/` only for a **real domain boundary** (coherent business capability, shared across routes).

Good: `user`, `billing`, `orders`, `notifications`, `authentication`.

Do not invent domains to avoid global files. Before creating a domain, ask: business ownership? multiple behaviors? shared routes? understandable boundary?

---

## 7. Be careful with domain separation

Do not force technical concepts into `features/` (`form`, `table`, `modal`, `date`, `validation`).

Prefer `components/ui/`, `lib/date/`, `lib/validation/`.

A **route** is not automatically a **domain** (`features/dashboard/` just because `/dashboard` exists).

---

## 8. Shared UI

Generic UI in `components/ui/` with minimal domain knowledge.

---

## 9. Shared hooks

Global `hooks/` = generic React only. Domain/page hooks stay page- or feature-scoped.

---

## 10. Shared helpers / utilities

Avoid a giant global `utils/` dump. Organize shared code by semantic purpose under `lib/`:

```text
lib/date/
lib/validation/
lib/api/
```

Prefer `@/lib/date/formatDateTime` over `@/utils`. Avoid `helpers/` without strong reason.

---

## 11. Shared constants

Global only when application-wide (`constants/routes.ts`). Domain constants in `features/<domain>/constants.ts`. Page constants in `app/<route>/constants.ts`.

---

## 12. Validation rules

Generic validation → `lib/validation/`. Business limits (e.g. max username length) → domain or page constants/schema. Ask: technical, domain, or page-specific?

---

## 13. Shared types

Prefer `features/<domain>/types.ts` or `app/<route>/types.ts`. Global `types/` only for truly cross-cutting abstractions (e.g. pagination).

---

## 14. Zustand stores

Store lives where state is owned: global UI → `stores/`; domain → `features/<domain>/`; page → `app/<route>/_stores/`.

---

## 15. Server Actions

Page-specific → `app/<route>/_actions/`. Domain → `features/<domain>/actions.ts`. Avoid a giant global `actions/` dump unless truly app-wide.

---

## 16. Avoid premature abstraction

Do not promote to shared scope because it might be reused, looks generic, or two files use it today. Progression: inline in page → `_components/` → `features/<domain>/` → generic `components/ui/` only with evidence.

---

## 17. Avoid junk drawer directories

Be careful with `utils/`, `helpers/`, `misc/`, `common/`, `shared/`, undifferentiated `types/`, `constants/`. Ask: **What owns this?** Never use `common/` or `shared/` as a dump.

---

## 18. Prefer semantic directories

Prefer `lib/date/`, `lib/validation/`, `lib/api/` over `lib/utils/`, `lib/helpers/`, `lib/common/`.

---

## 19. Placement over convenience

Do not ask “What type of file is this?” Ask **“Who owns this code?”** then pick page, domain, or shared tier.

---

## 20. Do not reorganize unnecessarily

Preserve coherent existing layout. No large moves for style alone. New code follows ownership rules. Fix placement only when already in that area and low-risk.

---

## 21. Final principle

> **Keep code as close as possible to the thing that owns it. Promote only when ownership genuinely broadens.**

```text
                    GLOBAL / GENERIC
                          │
             ┌────────────┼────────────┐
         components/    hooks/       lib/
                          │
                    DOMAIN / FEATURE
                    features/<domain>/
                          │
                     PAGE / ROUTE
                    app/<route>/
                 _components / _hooks / _utils / _actions
```

When uncertain, prefer the **narrower** scope. Do not create abstractions without evidence of real ownership.
